[Event "Lasker endgames: Take the opposition and go around"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2k5/8/8/3K4/2P5/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/H17vt8vC"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/2k5/8/8/3K4/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E01"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 53"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Lasker endgames. Learn opposition: move your king to c4, then go around. [%cal Yd3c4] } 1. Kc4 { Your king takes the opposition. His king must give way. [%cal Gd3c4] } (1. c4 { That lets him draw. Your pawn advances before the king makes room. } 1... Kb7 2. Ke4 2... Kc7 { His king can block the pawn. Bring your king forward first. [%cal Rb7c7] }) (1. Kd4 { Also winning. You can still use a spare pawn move later. } 1... Kd6 { Giving him the opposition here does not lose the win: your pawn still has a spare step. [%cal Bc6d6] }) 1... Kd6 { He steps aside. Move your king to b5 and go around him. [%cal Yc4b5] } 2. Kb5 { You outflanked him. Keep your king ahead of the pawn and work toward the sixth rank. [%cal Gc4b5] } (2. Kb3 { Also winning. Your king goes to b3. } 2... Kc7 { Also winning. Your king stays ahead, and the pawn move waits for a better moment. [%cal Bd6c7] }) (2. Kb4 { Also winning. Your king goes to b4. } 2... Kc7 { This wins too. Keep the king in front and hold the pawn move in reserve. [%cal Bd6c7] }) (2. Kc3 { This retreat gives away the win. } 2... Kc5 3. Kb3 3... Kb5 { His king can hold the opposition. Use the room you gained on the other side. [%cal Rc5b5] }) (2. Kd4 { Also winning. Your king goes to d4. } 2... Ke7 { Still winning: your king leads the way, with the pawn move saved for later. [%cal Bd6e7] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Choose a wing after taking opposition"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4k3/2p3p1/2P3P1/4K3/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/mlOgRUIi"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/4k3/2p3p1/2P3P1/4K3/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E02"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 60"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Both pawn pairs are locked. Can your king make his king give way? } 1. Ke4 { You take the opposition. His king cannot keep both wings covered. [%cal Ge3e4] } (1. Kd3 { That lets him draw. Your king heads to d3 instead. } 1... Kf6 2. Ke2 2... Ke7 { The win is gone: the pawns stay locked, and his king holds every entry square. [%cal Rf6e7] }) (1. Kf3 { That lets him draw. Your king goes to f3. } 1... Ke5 2. Ke3 2... Kf6 { This leaves the pawns locked. His king guards the entry squares, and the win is gone. [%cal Re5f6] }) 1... Kd6 { He chooses to guard one wing. } 2. Kf5 { You enter on the other side and threaten the g pawn. [%cal Ge4f5] } (2. Kd3 { This draws instead of winning. Your king tries d3. } 2... Kd7 3. Kc3 3... Kc6 { The win slips away: with the pawns still locked, his king covers the squares you needed. [%cal Rd7c6] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Bring the king back before pushing"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/6k1/8/7K/8/2P5/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/A2t5HfyC"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "6k1/8/7K/8/2P5/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E03"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 51"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Your king is far from its pawn. Can you bring it back while holding the sixth rank? } 1. Kg6 { Your king returns first. Pushing the pawn now would let his king catch it. } (1. c5 { That lets him draw. Your king must get back to the pawn first. } 1... Kf7 2. Kg5 2... Ke6 { His king can catch the pawn. Keep the pawn still while your king returns. [%cal Rf7e6] }) (1. Kg5 { That lets him draw. Your king goes to g5. } 1... Kg7 2. Kf5 2... Kf7 { The win is gone: his king holds firm, and the sixth-rank technique was the only road through. [%cal Rg7f7] }) 1... Kf8 { Black stays near the back rank. Keep your king in front of the pawn. } 2. Kf6 { Your king blocks his way down. The pawn can wait safely. } (2. c5 { That lets him draw. Your pawn pushes to c5 instead. } 2... Ke7 3. Kf5 3... Kd7 { This loses the win. His king holds the position that the correct technique would have denied him. [%cal Re7d7] }) (2. Kf5 { That lets him draw. Your king goes to f5. } 2... Kf7 3. c5 3... Ke7 { The win slips away here. His king keeps the position; go back to the lesson's technique instead. [%cal Rf7e7] }) 2... Ke8 { He gives way again. Can you keep your king on the sixth rank? } 3. Ke6 { Your king is back with the pawn. The sixth rank keeps his king away. [%tail] } (3. c5 { This only draws. The pawn tries c5 too early. } 3... Kd7 4. Ke5 4... Kc6 { This lets him hold the position, and the win is gone. [%cal Rd7c6] }) (3. Ke5 { That lets him draw. Your king goes to e5. } 3... Ke7 4. Kd5 4... Kd7 { The win is gone: his king keeps its grip, exactly what the sixth-rank technique was meant to prevent. [%cal Re7d7] }) 3... Kd8 4. Kd6 4... Ke8 5. c5 5... Kf7 6. c6 6... Kf6 7. c7 7... Kf5 8. c8=Q+ { Queen, with check! Your king led the pawn home exactly as the sixth-rank technique promised. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Choose the correct retreat"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/3k4/3P4/4K3/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_3"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/7IyMYzDT"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/3k4/3P4/4K3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 3"]
[ChapterId "E04"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 50"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ The pawn reached the sixth rank. Which retreat keeps its promotion square under control? } 3... Kd8 { You step straight back toward the pawn road, saving the right opposition. [%cal Gd7d8] } (3... Kc8 { That loses. Your king heads to c8 instead. } 4. Ke6 4... Kd8 5. d7 5... Kc7 6. Ke7 { This retreat loses the opposition, and White walks the pawn in for promotion. [%cal Re6e7] }) (3... Ke8 { That loses. Your king goes to e8. } 4. Ke6 4... Kd8 5. d7 5... Kc7 6. Ke7 { The wrong square costs you the opposition. White escorts the pawn home from here. [%cal Re6e7] }) 4. Ke6 { White brings the king forward. } 4... Ke8 { You take the opposition now, not one move too early. [%cal Gd8e8] } (4... Kc8 { This loses the draw. Your king tries c8. } 5. Ke7 5... Kb7 6. d7 { Losing the opposition here lets White's king shepherd the pawn to promotion. [%cal Rd6d7] }) 5. d7+ { The pawn advances with check. } 5... Kd8 { You return in front of the pawn. A king move to d6 would leave you stalemated. [%cal Ge8d8] } (5... Kf8 { That loses: you leave the promotion square unguarded. } 6. Kf6 6... Kg8 7. Kg6 7... Kf8 8. d8=Q# { Mate. The wrong retreat let his king escort the pawn to promotion. [%cal Rd7d8] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Take opposition from far away"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/8/1p5p/1P5P/8/3K4/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/4dGrXtVG"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4k3/8/8/1p5p/1P5P/8/3K4/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E05"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Distant opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 62"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Distant opposition works too. Move your king to e2, matching the distant king file. [%cal Yd2e2] } 1. Ke2 { Your kings share a file, with an odd number of squares between them. [%cal Gd2e2] } (1. Kd3 { The king to d3 only draws here. } 1... Kd7 2. Ke3 2... Ke7 { The win is gone: he takes the opposition and blocks your path to the pawns. [%cal Rd7e7] }) (1. Ke3 { That lets him draw. Your king heads to e3 instead. } 1... Ke7 2. Kf3 2... Kf7 { This hands him the opposition, and with it the win. He blocks your way to the pawns. [%cal Re7f7] }) 1... Kd7 { He steps toward the center. Move your king to d3 to keep the opposition. [%cal Ye2d3] } 2. Kd3 { You meet him on the same file. [%cal Ge2d3] } (2. Ke3 { This only draws. Your king tries e3. } 2... Ke7 3. Kf3 3... Kf7 { The win is gone here. His king takes the opposition and shuts you out of the pawns. [%cal Re7f7] }) 2... Ke7 { He changes files. Move your king to e3 and keep him facing you. [%cal Yd3e3] } 3. Ke3 { You follow across and keep the opposition. [%cal Gd3e3] } (3. Kd4 { That lets him draw. Your king goes to d4. } 3... Kd6 4. Ke4 4... Ke6 { He gets the opposition this way, and the win is gone with it. [%cal Rd6e6] }) 3... Kd6 { He steps closer. Move your king to d4 and take direct opposition. [%cal Ye3d4] } 4. Kd4 { The opposition is now direct. His king must give way to one wing. [%cal Ge3d4] } (4. Ke4 { That lets him draw. Your king goes to e4. } 4... Ke6 5. Kd4 5... Kd6 { The win slips away: his king holds the opposition and bars your path to the pawns. [%cal Re6d6] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Do not rush toward the pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/k1p5/2P1K2p/7P/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/qYdJRKeU"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/k1p5/2P1K2p/7P/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E06"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Distant opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 67"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Rushing at his pawn can lose yours. Can you take distant opposition first? } 1. Ke5 { You take distant opposition. His king must yield a way toward the c5 pawn. [%tail] [%cal Ge4e5] } (1. Kd5 { That loses. Your king heads to d5 instead. } 1... Kb4 2. Ke4 2... Kxc4 { Your c pawn falls, and Black wins the pawn race that follows. [%cal Rb4c4] }) (1. Kf5 { That loses. Your king goes to f5. } 1... Kb4 2. Kg5 2... Kxc4 3. Kxh4 { This drops the c pawn, and Black wins the race that is left. [%cal Rg5h4] }) 1... Ka6 2. Kd5 2... Kb6 3. Kd6 3... Kb7 4. Kxc5 { Your king captures the pawn. Up a pawn with the more active king, his h pawn is already blockaded. [%cal Gd6c5] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Save a pawn step to gain a turn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/3k4/1p6/1P1K4/8/2P5/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/ghkBBhiX"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/3k4/1p6/1P1K4/8/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E07"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Reserve tempi"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 57"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A reserve tempo is a spare pawn step. Push your c pawn just one square, to c3. [%cal Yc2c3] } 1. c3 { You spend one pawn step and make his king move first. [%cal Gc2c3] } (1. c4 { That gives away the win. You use both pawn steps at once. } 1... bxc4 2. Kxc4 2... Kc6 { The exchanges give him a drawn king-and-pawn ending. Keep one step in reserve. [%cal Rd6c6] }) (1. Kc3 { Also winning. Your king goes to c3. } 1... Ke5 { Also winning. The spare pawn move is still sitting in reserve for later. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (1. Kd3 { Also winning. Your king can also try d3. } 1... Ke5 { This wins too, and the spare pawn move is still available whenever you need it. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (1. Ke3 { Also winning. Your king can also head to e3. } 1... Ke5 { Still winning: you have not spent the reserve pawn move yet. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (1. Ke4 { Also winning. Your spare c pawn step has not disappeared. } 1... Ke6 { You can spend the spare pawn move later. This line just uses it right away. [%cal Bd6e6] }) 1... Kc6 { He steps aside. Move your king to e5 and take the space. [%cal Yd4e5] } 2. Ke5 { Your spare pawn step made him yield. Now your king can work around toward b5. [%cal Gd4e5] } (2. Kd3 { That retreat gives up the win and lets him draw. } 2... Kd5 3. Ke2 3... Kd6 { He restores the opposition. Use the space your pawn move created. [%cal Rd5d6] }) (2. Ke3 { This wins too. Your king tries e3 instead. } 2... Kd5 { Also winning. That pawn move is still in your pocket for the right moment. [%cal Bc6d5] }) (2. Ke4 { Also winning. Your king can also head to e4. } 2... Kc7 { This also wins, and the spare tempo is still there to use later. [%cal Bc6c7] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Spend the reserve pawn move"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3k4/8/3K4/2P5/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/f8MYotnk"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "3k4/8/3K4/2P5/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E08"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Reserve tempi"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 52"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Your king is ahead. Can the spare pawn step make his king move? } 1. c6 { You spend the spare move. His king must give up the opposition. [%cal Gc5c6] } (1. Kc6 { Also winning. You approach with the king before spending the pawn move. } 1... Ke7 { You still have the spare pawn move. This line just spends it right away. [%cal Bd8e7] }) 1... Kc8 { He steps aside. } 2. c7 { Your pawn reaches the seventh under king protection. [%cal Gc6c7] } (2. Kd5 { This retreat only draws. } 2... Kc7 3. Kc5 3... Kc8 { His king blocks the pawn. Keep your king in front and push the pawn. [%cal Rc7c8] }) 2... Kb7 { He moves away from promotion. } 3. Kd7 { Your king guards the promotion square. The pawn can become a queen. [%cal Gd6d7] } (3. Ke6 { That gives away the pawn and the win. } 3... Kxc7 { He takes your last pawn. Draw. Guard the pawn as well as its promotion square. [%cal Rb7c7] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: A checking pawn sacrifice saves the draw"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4kp2/8/5PP1/4K3/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/tJsY2i42"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/4kp2/8/5PP1/4K3/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E09"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Reserve tempi"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 69"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ You are a pawn down. White will bring the king forward; look for a saving pawn move. } 1. Ke4 { White takes the opposition. Can your pawn interrupt the king invasion? } 1... f5+ { Your pawn checks and offers an exchange. [%cal Gf6f5] } (1... Kd6 { That loses. Your king heads to d6 instead. } 2. Kf5 2... Ke7 3. Kg6 { This lets White outflank your king and walk a pawn home. Stay beside the pawns with the opposition. [%cal Rf5g6] }) (1... Ke7 { That loses. Your king goes to e7. } 2. Kd5 2... Kd7 3. f5 { White's king slips around yours and escorts a pawn in. Hold the opposition next to the pawns instead. [%cal Rf4f5] }) 2. gxf5+ { White accepts and checks your king. } 2... Kf6 { You take the opposition beside the remaining pawn. [%cal Ge6f6] } (2... Kd6 { This loses. Your king tries d6. } 3. Kd4 3... Kc6 4. Ke5 { Losing contact here lets White outflank you and escort a pawn through. Keep the opposition by the pawns. [%cal Rd4e5] }) 3. Kd5 { White moves the king. } 3... Kxf5 { You take the front pawn. Your king stands in front of the last pawn to hold the draw. [%cal Gf6f5] } (3... Kf7 { That loses. Take the front pawn while it is unprotected. } 4. Ke5 4... Ke8 5. f6 { White keeps the dangerous pawn. Your king needed to collect it immediately. [%cal Rf5f6] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Create a distant passed pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1pp1k2p/P5p1/1PP3P1/5K2/8/8_b_-_-_0_2"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/RwsQuE6K"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/1pp1k2p/P5p1/1PP3P1/5K2/8/8 b - - 0 2"]
[ChapterId "E10"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Outside passed pawns"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 59, after 1.a4 Ke6 2.a5"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A distant passed pawn pulls his king away. Watch the capture, then create your passer. } 2... bxa5 { He takes the a pawn. Recapture with your b pawn to make a distant passer. [%cal Yb4a5] } 3. bxa5 { Your passed a pawn distracts his king. Your king can head for g5 and h6. [%tail] [%cal Gf3e4,Gb4a5] } (3. b5 { That loses. Your pawn goes to b5. } 3... a4 4. bxc6 4... Kd6 { His a pawn runs free this way. Recapture on a5 before any new pawn race. [%cal Re6d6] }) (3. c5 { That loses. Your pawn goes to c5. } 3... a4 { He pushes the outside passer instead of taking b4. Your king cannot catch it. } 4. Ke4 4... a3 { This lets the a pawn run free. Take on a5 first, before racing pawns. [%cal Ra4a3] }) (3. Ke4 { That loses. Your king goes to e4. } 3... axb4 4. Kd4 4... Ke7 { You lose the b pawn instead of making a passer. Recapture on a5. [%cal Re6e7] }) 3... Kd6 4. Ke4 4... Kc5 5. Kf5 5... Kd4 6. a6 6... Ke3 7. a7 7... Kd4 8. a8=Q { Queen! Your distant passer promotes while his king is still stuck in the middle. [%csl Ga8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Win the race back to the other wing"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/p4k2/1p5p/1P3P1K/P7/8/8_b_-_-_1_6"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/3w93nEfY"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/p4k2/1p5p/1P3P1K/P7/8/8 b - - 1 6"]
[ChapterId "E11"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Outside passed pawns"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 58"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Both kings will collect a passed pawn. Can you finish closer to the remaining pawns? } 6... Kf5 { Your king approaches the f pawn from the side nearest the queen side. [%cal Gf6f5] } (6... Kg6 { Also winning. Your king can still collect the f pawn. } 7. Kh3 { You still win the race back. Your direct route stays closer to the queen side. [%cal Bh4h3] }) 7. Kxh5 { White takes your distant h pawn. } 7... Kxf4 { You take his passer and finish nearer the remaining pawns. [%cal Gf5f4] } (7... Ke6 { That loses. You turn away before taking the passed pawn. } 8. Kg5 8... Kd5 9. Kf6 { White can escort the f pawn. Collect that passer before going to the other wing. [%cal Rg5f6] }) 8. Kg6 { His king starts the long return trip. } 8... Ke5 { Your king heads toward the queen side with a useful head start. [%cal Gf4e5] } (8... Ke3 { Still winning: e3 works for your king as well. } 9. Kf7 { Also winning. Your king still wins the race back to the queen side. [%cal Bg6f7] }) (8... Ke4 { This wins too. Your king tries e4 instead. } 9. Kf6 { This wins too. You still beat him back to the queen side. [%cal Bg6f6] }) (8... Kf3 { Also winning. Your king goes to f3. } 9. Kf7 { Still winning: your king still arrives on the queen side first. [%cal Bg6f7] }) (8... Kg4 { That loses the king race. Your king heads the wrong way. } 9. Kf7 9... Kf5 10. Ke7 { White can reach the remaining pawns. Use your head start toward the queen side. [%cal Rf7e7] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Give a pawn to gain the opposition"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/p1k3p1/P5P1/1PK5/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/tmOyCXcA"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/p1k3p1/P5P1/1PK5/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E12"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Pawn sacrifices and breakthroughs"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 68"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A pawn sacrifice can win opposition. Push your b pawn to b4 with check. [%cal Yb3b4] } 1. b4+ { Your sacrifice pulls his pawn off a5 and creates a useful king route. [%cal Gb3b4] } (1. Kb2 { Also winning. Your king goes to b2. } 1... Kd4 { Also winning. The b pawn lever is still there while your king improves. [%cal Bc5d4] }) (1. Kc2 { Also winning. Your king can maneuver before using the pawn lever. } 1... Kd5 { Your king can maneuver and still use the b pawn lever later. [%cal Bc5d5] }) (1. Kd2 { Also winning. Your king goes to d2. } 1... Kd4 { This wins too, and the b pawn break is still available once your king is better placed. [%cal Bc5d4] }) (1. Kd3 { This wins too. Your king heads to d3 instead. } 1... Kd5 { Still winning: you can improve your king first and keep the b pawn lever in hand. [%cal Bc5d5] }) 1... axb4+ { He accepts with check. Move your king to b3 before taking the pawn. [%cal Yc3b3] } 2. Kb3 { Your king steps onto b3, ready to collect the pawn from a better square. [%cal Gc3b3] } (2. Kd3 { That gives away the win. Your king chooses the wrong side of the pawn. } 2... Kd5 3. Kc2 3... Kd6 { Black can hold the opposition. Step to b3 before collecting the pawn. [%cal Rd5d6] }) 2... Kd6 { His king moves away. Take the pawn on b4 with your king. [%cal Yb3b4] } 3. Kxb4 { You recover the pawn with your king in front. The sacrifice bought position. [%cal Gb3b4] } (3. a5 { Also winning, but you leave the loose b pawn for later. } 3... Ke5 { You can still win. Taking the loose pawn immediately makes the plan much clearer. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (3. Kc4 { Also winning. Your king goes to c4. } 3... Ke5 { Also winning. Your king can still collect the b4 pawn and escort your passer. [%cal Bd6e5] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: A rook for connected passed pawns"]
