{ Can giving up your rook stop his passer and set your own pawns free? } 1... Rxd7 { You remove the dangerous passer before starting your own pawn race. [%cal Gd5d7] } (1... g3 { That loses. The rook can check before your pawns get organized. } 2. Rh6+ 2... Kc5 3. Rxh3 3... Kc4 4. Rxg3 4... Rxd7 5. Kxd7 { White wins material with rook checks. Remove the d pawn with your rook first. [%cal Rc8d7] }) 2. Rxd7 { White must use the rook: your king guards d7. } 2... g3 { Both passers are on the sixth rank. Here one rook cannot stop them both. [%cal Gg4g3] } (2... h2 { Also winning. Start with the h pawn and support it with the g pawn. } 3. Rh7 3... g3 { Your g pawn now guards h2. This move order also wins. [%cal Bg4g3] }) 0-1


